    <h1 class="display-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="color:#fff;max-width:880px;">Concept before execution: decisions are the service, not drafts</h1>
    <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.88);max-width:720px;margin-top:1rem;">What to do is the hard question. What it looks like is the follow-up question.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:4rem 2rem;max-width:760px;">
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Execution is no longer a scarce resource. Design, copy, images, layout, code — all of this has become fast and cheap in recent years, and the trend will not reverse. An agency that positions itself on execution positions itself against a competitor that never tires and costs almost nothing.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What is not available automatically is the decision about <em>what</em> should be executed — and the responsibility for that decision. That is why at Lünstroth diagnosis and concept come first, rather than running alongside as unpaid groundwork.</p>
  <p class="pull-quote reveal reveal-delay-2">Diagnosis and concept are services in their own right with a defined scope — not unbilled groundwork for a design commission.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What a concept is — and what it is not</h2>
  <div class="compare-grid reveal reveal-delay-1">
    <div class="compare-col">
      <span class="compare-tag">What is often called a concept</span>
      <p class="compare-text" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">A collection of ideas. A mood board. Three proposals to choose from. A presentation that shows possibilities and hands the decision back to the client.</p>
      <p class="compare-text">The tell-tale sign: there is no rationale that could be checked. So taste gets negotiated — and the approval committee decides by what it likes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-col">
      <span class="compare-tag">What concept means here</span>
      <p class="compare-text" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">A well-founded decision about what is done and what is left out, with a rationale that can be traced from the decision situation through to the means.</p>
      <p class="compare-text">The tell-tale sign: you can disagree with it. Not because you dislike something, but because an assumption is wrong. That is a productive argument.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:760px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Change follows strategy</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The primacy of the concept leads to a second principle that runs against expectations even more often in practice: what the strategy demands is changed — nothing more. Recognition is an economic asset built up over years, and giving it up means writing it off. You do that for a reason or not at all.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Classic design studios often start thinking about a new logo before anyone has asked for one — a relaunch is a visible result and easy to sell. The analysis, however, regularly leads to a different answer:</p>
  <ul class="check-list">
    {%- for item in [
      "Gneuss: the logo was not the problem, a single element of it was. The symbol went, the word mark stayed unchanged.",
      "Volksbank PHD: the acquired local brand was kept in full. An endorsement instead of a merger — dissolving it would have cost more than it brought.",
      "Cobiax: here the change was genuinely necessary. And even then the characteristic extension of the “x” was kept.",
      "Meco: adapted to digital viewing habits without leaving the existing character of the logo."
    ] %}
    <li class="check-item reveal reveal-delay-{{ loop.index0 % 3 }}">
      <span class="check-icon"></span>
      <span>{{ item }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Four mandates, four different depths of intervention — and in three of the four, the most valuable part of the brand was left untouched. That is no coincidence but the result of the same question: does the existing form still carry the strategy?</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The four stages — and why the order is not negotiable</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">Each stage can be commissioned individually. None can be skipped without the next one building on assumptions instead of findings.</p>
  <div class="grid-2 reveal reveal-delay-1" style="gap:clamp(1.5rem,2.5vw,2.5rem);">
    {%- for s in [
      {n:"A", t:"Diagnosis", d:"Market position, competitive environment, sales channels, pricing rationale, legal situation. The result is a description of the status quo that includes uncomfortable findings too. Fixed scope, can be commissioned on its own."},
      {n:"B", t:"Concept", d:"The well-founded decision: what to do, what to leave out, in which order, with what expected effect. This is also where the depth of intervention in the existing brand is decided."},
      {n:"C", t:"Execution", d:"Brand and identity, media that sell: packaging, sales and listing documents, website, video, specialist communication. Every medium follows from the concept and can be checked against it."},
      {n:"D", t:"Operation", d:"Ongoing care of the brand system, content and product data, brand rules for the client's AI systems, technical operation. A brand that is not maintained falls apart into its components."}
    ] %}
    <div>
